@page "/mechanism/1-ijsruntime"

<PageTitle>1. IJSRuntime — JS interop demos</PageTitle>

<div class="mechanism-page">
    <MechanismNav />

    <header class="mech-head">
        <p class="mech-kicker">
            Mechanism 1 of @Mechanisms.All.Count · you are reading this in
            <span class="host-chip">@RuntimeMode.Name</span> — the same page in
            <CounterpartLink />
        </p>
        <h1>IJSRuntime: the classic string-based call</h1>
        <p class="mech-lede">
            The first thing everyone learns: name the function in a string, pass arguments, get a
            result back. There is nothing to set up and nothing to generate, and the same two lines
            run on both hosting models.
        </p>
        <p class="demo-support">
            Available in: <strong>@Mechanisms.Availability(1)</strong>
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What actually crosses the boundary</h2>
        <p>
            <code>IJSRuntime</code> resolves the name against the <strong>global scope</strong> of the
            page. On WebAssembly that global is the browser's own. On Blazor Server the call is
            serialised into a message, sent over the circuit, executed against the browser's global
            scope, and the result comes back as another message — which is why every method on this
            interface is asynchronous and why a server-side call has a network hop in it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Arguments and the return value travel as JSON. A <code>string</code> in C# becomes a JSON
            string, is parsed into a JavaScript value, and the result is written back out again. The
            function name itself is only a string, so nothing about a wrong name can be a build error.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What the panels print in @RuntimeMode.Name</h2>
        <p class="mech-out-label">✓ One call into a window global</p>
        <pre class="mech-out">Hello, Blazor — this string came out of a window global.</pre>

        <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Typo the function name — <em>demoGreeterTwo</em> does not exist</p>
        <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">JSException: The value 'demoGreeterTwo' is not a function.
Error: The value 'demoGreeterTwo' is not a function.</pre>

        <p class="mech-note">
            Recorded from this build, and identical on both hosts: the message names the string you
            passed and says it is not a function. Notice what it does <strong>not</strong> say — the
            runtime cannot tell you that you meant <em>demoGreeter</em>, and it only knows the name is
            wrong once the call runs, on the user's machine.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-split">
        <div class="mech-use">
            <h2>Use it when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You need a single call with no setup, or you are prototyping.</li>
                <li>The JavaScript really is a global — a library that installed itself on
                    <code>window</code> before your component existed.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mech-never">
            <h2>Never when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You call the same function more than a few times. The serialisation adds up.</li>
                <li>You have more than two or three functions. That is a module pretending to be
                    globals.</li>
                <li>You want the compiler involved at all. It cannot check a string.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="mech-live-head">The live panels</h2>
    <CascadingValue Name="MechanismPageOwnsHeading" Value="true">
        <Demo01Globals />
    </CascadingValue>

    <MechanismPager Number="1" />
</div>
